## 1. Introduction
Advanced energy conversion technologies that are sustainable and environmentally friendly are highly required in order to meet the dramatically growing energy demand and reduce the dependence on combustion of non-renewable fossil fuels.[1-7] Many promising energy conversion technologies including fuel cells, water splitting, and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction have been extensively investigated and developed in recent years.[7-16] The development of these technologies relies heavily on exploration of efficient and durable electrocatalysts or photocatalysts for some key reactions, including oxygen reduction reaction (ORR), oxygen evolution reaction (OER), hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction reaction $(C O_{2}\mathsf{R R})$ . Tremendous efforts have been devoted to the design and synthesis of advanced materials for catalysis of these key energy conversion reactions and great progress has been made. [17-27] However, materials that can catalyze these energy conversion reactions in an efficient, durable, environmentally friendly, and low-cost way are still absent.
As a new type of crystalline porous materials, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and their derived materials have also been widely employed for these energy conversion reactions due to their unique characteristics including the organic-inorganic hybrid nature, high surface area, well-defined porosity, tunable chemical composition, and adjustable functionality.[28-39] It has been shown that the atomically dispersed metal sites (ADMSs) in pristine MOFs and MOF-derived materials played very important roles on both photocatalysis and electrocatalysis of the energy conversion reactions. For electrocatalytic energy conversion applications, the ADMSs can not only serve as active sites to adsorb and activate the reactive molecules (e.g. $H_{2}O$ , $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ ), but also act as redox active sites to promote the reduction or oxidation of the adsorbed molecules. For photocatalytic energy conversion applications, well-studied homogeneous metal complexes can be uniformly anchored or encaged in the nitrogen-containing ligands or pores in pristine MOFs to afford heterogeneous ADMSs incorporated in MOFs, which can serve as photosensitizers or catalysts for efficient and durable energy conversion photocatalysis.
ADMSs can be easily introduced in the metal nodes, organic ligands, or pores of pristine MOFs because of the highly ordered arrangement of organic ligands and metal nodes as well as the well-defined pore structure of MOFs. Moreover, the uniformly distributed ADMSs in pristine MOFs can be converted to ADMSs anchored in MOF-derived carbon materials through a simple heat treatment, which have higher electrical conductivity and consequently better performance for electrocatalytic energy conversion. Although many reviews have discussed the important roles of ADMSs in pristine MOFs for many applications including asymmetric reactions,[40] hydrogen storage,[41] $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture and conversion,[42] organic synthesis and transformations,[38, 43-45] and biomimetic catalysis,[46] a review focusing on the important role of ADMSs on energy conversion applications is still absent. Moreover, it is not until recent few years that ADMSs in MOFderived carbon materials have been synthesized and investigated in details, which initiates a new direction and is now a very hot topic in the field of MOF-derived materials. Therefore, a thorough review on recent progress of design and incorporation of ADMSs in MOFs and MOF-derived materials for energy conversion applications is highly desirable. Herein, we summarized recent progress and development of synthesis of ADMSs in MOFs and MOF-derived materials, mainly focusing on the role of these ADMSs for typical energy conversion applications including electro-/photocatalytic OER, HER, $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ , and ORR.
Different types of ADMSs in pristine MOFs and MOF-derived materials will be included for discussion in this review (Figure 1). For ADMSs in metal nodes, unsaturated metal sites that can adsorb and activate the reactive molecules are desirable. Unsaturated ADMSs can be produced in MOFs with intrinsic open metal sites after removal of the solvent molecules adsorbed on the metal sites (e.g. HKUST-1, MIL-101, and MOF-74), while unsaturated ADMSs can be also created on the surface of MOFs without intrinsic open metal sites by constructing ultrathin morphology due to the termination of organic ligands. ADMSs in the organic ligands of pristine MOFs are usually obtained using nitrogen-containing ligands that can coordinate with the metal sites (e.g. porphyrin-based and bipyridine-based ligands) as linkers. Besides metal nodes and organic ligands, ADMSs can be also afforded through encapsulation of the metal sites within the well-defined pores of MOFs. Cage-like pores with suitable pore diameters can lead to confinement of one metal complex within one pore. ADMSs in pristine MOFs can be converted to MOFderived carbon materials doped with ADMSs that are usually coordinated and stabilized by the nitrogen atoms in carbon matrix. The conversion is usually accomplished through high temperature pyrolysis and, if necessary, subsequent acid leaching of the aggregated metal materials.
ADMSs incorporated in MOFs and their derived carbon matrix can exhibit many merits and superiorities compared with homogeneous metal complexes or traditional heterogeneous catalysts like metal clusters/materials. Compared with homogeneous metal complexes, incorporation of ADMSs in MOFs to afford heterogeneous catalysts has the advantage of easy separation and good recyclability. Moreover, more than one kind of ADMSs or other components (e.g. polyoxometalates and Pt nanomaterials) can be incorporated into one MOF crystal to afford multifunctional heterogeneous catalyst. The intimate contact between the different components in MOFs can result in positive synergistic effects and facile electron transfer between the photosensitizer and catalyst components, leading to much enhanced photocatalytic energy conversion performance. Additionally, the immobilization of ADMSs in the robust frameworks of MOFs can ensure the isolation of the ADMSs from each other, which can prevent the degradation of the metal sites caused by interaction between multiple metal sites and lead to higher stability. Compared with metal cluster/materials, ADMSs anchored in MOF-derived carbon materials have merits of high catalytic activity and selectivity due to the $100\%$ atom utilization and well-defined configuration of the ADMSs. Also, mechanism study and simulation work of ADMSs in MOF-derived materials are facile to do because of the well-defined coordination environment of the single metal sites in carbon matrix.
There are many important characterization techniques for identification of the ADMSs in MOF-based materials, including aberration-corrected high-angle annular dark field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM), electron energy loss spectroscopy (EELS), X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS) measurement, and so on. Aberration-corrected HAADFSTEM with atomic resolution can directly visualize the ADMSs by the contrast originated from the Z difference between the metal sites and the substrates, and the chemical composition of the ADMSs (e.g. Ni, Fe, N) can be further identified by EELS attached to the microscopy. XAFS measurement can provide more detailed information of the ADMSs in terms of the oxidation states, coordination numbers, and coordination configurations of the metal sites, which is a very powerful technique to get insight into the catalytically active sites and catalytic mechanisms when combined with density functional theory (DFT) calculations.
Before the detailed discussion of ADMSs in MOF-based materials for energy conversion, a brief introduction of the development of ADMSs for various applications is presented in order to offer readers a broader overview of this hot field. Catalysts with ADMSs have recently attracted tremendous research interests because of their unique chemical properties, and the past decade has witnessed great progress and breakthroughs in design and synthesis of catalysts with ADMSs. Various ADMSs supported on various substrates, such as $\mathsf{P t}_{1}/\mathsf{F e O}_{\mathsf{x}}$ ,[47] $\mathfrak{I r}_{1}/\mathfrak{F e O}_{\times}.$ [48] Pt/TiN,[49] and $\mathsf{P d}_{1}/\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ ,[50] have been successfully synthesized and characterized, which exhibited excellent catalytic performances towards various reactions such as CO oxidation and water gas shift reaction. The superb catalytic performances of the ADMSs catalysts can be attributed to their $100\%$ atom utilization, unsaturated coordination configuration of the metal sites, and the strong metal-support interactions that lead to charge transfer between the metal sites and supports.[51-52] Although many synthetic methods, including wet-impregnation method, atomic layer deposition (ALD) method, and atom trapping method, have been developed to fabricate catalysts with
ADMSs, their efficient, simple, and scalable synthesis still remains a great challenge. As a new kind of porous materials, MOFs have recently attracted increasing attention in this field as MOFs serve as emerging and promising platforms for design and synthesis of heterogeneous catalysts with ADMSs, which will be discussed in detail in the following sections.
In the following sections, recent progress and development of utilization of these MOF-based materials incorporated with ADMSs for advanced electrocatalytic and photocatalytic energy conversion applications will be summarized, which are classified according to different types of ADMSs in pristine MOFs and MOFderived materials, respectively. Hopefully this review can provide the readers a broad overview and comprehensive understanding of the important role of the ADMSs incorporated in pristine MOFs and MOF-derived materials on various energy conversion applications, and inspires more insightful researches and significant breakthroughs in this field.
