## 2. ADMSs in pristine MOFs
MOFs have highly ordered arrangements of the metal nodes and organic ligands, making it possible to afford ADMSs in pristine MOFs by incorporations of metal sites in the metal nodes, organic ligands, and well-defined pores. In the case of metal nodes, intrinsically exposed open metal sites are presented in many MOFs (e.g. HKUST-1 and MIL-101), while unsaturated metal sites can be also generated by constructing ultrathin 2D morphologies of MOFs without intrinsically open metal sites. These coordinatively unsaturated metal sites play an essential role for energy conversion catalysis, which can serve as adsorbing sites for adsorption and activation of the reactant molecules. To afford ADMSs in organic ligands, utilization of organic ligands with nitrogen (e.g. porphyrin- and bipyridinebased ligands) to anchor the metal sites is a commonly used strategy as various ADMSs, including Ru, Pt, Ir, Re, Mn, Fe, Co sites and so on, can be incorporated in the organic ligands through the coordination bonding between the nitrogen and the metal sites. The well-defined pore structures of MOFs enable the efficient confinement of metal complexes within the pores, leading to heterogeneous catalysts with uniformly dispersed metal sites that are comparable to and even better than the homogeneous counterparts in terms of activity and stability for energy conversion catalysis. The following sections will present recent progress of pristine MOFs with ADMSs in the metal nodes, linkers, and pores for energy conversion catalysis, focusing on revealing the important role of ADMSs both experimentally and theoretically. Table 1 summarized typical ADMS-incorporated pristine MOFs for energy conversion catalysis.
2.1. ADMSs in metal nodes of pristine MOFs
ADMSs exist in the metal nodes of most of the MOFs, as the highly ordered arrangements of the metal nodes and organic ligands lead to uniformly distribution of the metal sites isolated by organic ligands throughout the whole MOF crystal. ADMSs in metal nodes with coordinatively unsaturated sites and redox activities are highly desirable for energy conversion catalysis, which can adsorb and activate the reactive molecules and promote the charge transfer during the reactions.
## 2.1.1. Co sites
Homogeneous and heterogeneous Co-based materials have been widely employed for various energy conversion applications due to the rich redox chemistry, low cost, high relative abundance, and high stability in different conditions.[62-65]  Compared with other Co-based materials, Co-based MOFs exhibit unique catalytic properties by virtue of high surface area, facile modular design and synthesis, and easily adjustable coordination environment of the active Co sites. Co-based MOFs with active Co sites can be constructed using paddle-wheel type di-cobalt metal nodes ${\mathsf{C o}}_{2}({\mathsf{R C O O}})_{4}$ $\overset{\cdot}{\mathsf{R}}=\mathsf{\Omega}$ substituent group) with novel square-planar coordination geometry, which however have very poor stabilities in aqueous solution so that their applications for energy conversion are limited.[66] To address this issue, Shen and co-workers developed a modular synthesis strategy to stabilize the di-cobalt metal sites using a stable iron carboxylate framework $\mathsf{F e}_{3}(\upmu_{3}-\mathsf{O})(\mathsf{b d c})_{3}$ $(\pmb{\mathsf{F e}}_{3})$ as host to immobilize the $\mathsf{C o}_{2}(\mathsf{R C O O})_{4}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{2}$ clusters (Figure 2A-C).[67] Fe3 frameworks have arrangement of open iron sites which can perfectly fit and anchor the bowl-shaped tetradentate metalloligands composed of four nicotinate (na−) ligands and a paddle-wheel type $\mathsf{C o}_{2}(\mathsf{R C O O})_{4}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{2}$ core. The resultant hybrid MOFs {Fe3(μ3- $\mathsf{O})(\mathsf{b d c})_{3}\}_{4}\{\mathsf{C o}_{2}(\mathsf{n a})_{4}(\mathsf{L}^{\top})_{2}\}_{3}\ (\mathsf{F e}_{3}-\mathsf{C o}_{2})$ consisting of the stable Fe3 frameworks and the catalytically active di-cobalt metal sites exhibited excellent stability in aqueous solution, which can remain intact at $p H=13$ after $24\mathsf{h}$ . When employed as electrocatalyst for OER, it showed excellent activity with onset potential of $1.46\mathrm{~V~}$ and overpotential of $283m v$ at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ along with high durability during OER electrocatalysis in water at both $p H=13$ and $p H=7$ . This research provides a new design idea to immobilize and stabilize the active but labile metal sites using stable frameworks of MOFs through modular synthesis to afford catalysts with both high activity and stability.
Another solution is enhancing the activity of the Co sites of Co-based MOFs with high chemical stability but low catalytic stability. Similar to homogeneous cobalt complexes, the coordination environment of cobalt sites in MOFs has great influences on their catalytic performances for energy conversion applications, which can be finely tuned through postsynthetic ion exchange. For example, Lu and co-workers successfully modified the coordination environment of the open cobalt sites of a Cobased MOF, MAF-X27-Cl, by replacing the Cl- ligands with OHthrough repeated LSV performed at $p H=14$ or immersion in 1 M KOH for $24\ h$ (Figure 2D-H).[68] After the ${\mathsf{O H}}^{-}$ modification of the Co sites, the resultant MOF, MAF-X27-OH, showed much enhanced electrocatalytic OER performance with overpotential of $387\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}$ at $10\mathsf{m A}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , which was 121 mV lower than that of MAFX27-Cl before OH- modification, along with excellent durability during OER electrocatalysis at $p H=14$ . The significantly improved OER performance is attributed to the OER process with lower energy barrier through the intraframework coupling pathway involving the direct participation of the $\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\left(\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\right)\mathsf{I}\mathsf{\Pi}\left(\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\right).$ ligands of MAF-X27- OH.
In other cases, the important roles of Co sites in metal nodes of ZIF-9 on electrocatalysis of OER[69] and photocatalysis of $C O_{2}R R^{[70]}$ were demonstrated. For OER, it was suggested that water molecule is first bound to the Co active site of ZIF-9 and the benzimidazole ligands around the Co site can facilitate the direct abstraction of a H atom from the water molecule bound to the Co active site and promote the decomposition of water. For $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ , it was suggested that the synergistic effect between Co sites and the benzimidazole ligands can greatly improve the photocatalytic performance for $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ , leading to a TON of $\mathord{\sim}450$ for $2.5\mathsf{h}$ under mild reaction conditions.
