## 2.1.2. Cu sites
In addition to Co, MOFs with Cu metal sites in metal nodes were also utilized for energy conversion. HKUST-1, one of the most studied Cu-based MOFs, were employed as catalysts for photocatalytic $C O_{2}R R$ by Li and co-workers (Figure 3A-C).[71] HKUST-1 has desirable $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption capacity as well as a large amount of open Cu sites which can act as catalytic sites, rendering it an ideal catalyst for $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ . HKUST- $1@\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ coreshell structure with the semiconductor $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ shell as photosensitizer and the HKUST-1 core as catalyst was constructed to facilitate the photoelectron transfer from $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ to HKUST-1 core, where the selective reduction of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to $C H_{4}$ took place. The facile charge transfer from photoexcited $\Gamma_{1}0_{2}$ to HKUST-1, which can not only suppress the electron-hole recombination but also provide sufficient electron to the Cu sites for activation and selective reduction of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecules, was demonstrated using ultrafast spectroscopy in this research. As a result, HKUST- $1@\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ delivered excellent photocatalytic performance for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction with a $C H_{4}$ production rate of 2.64 μmol $9\pi{\tt o}_{2}^{-1}~{\sf h}^{-1}$ without side product ${\sf H}_{2}$ , while no product was detected for bare HKUST-1 under the same condition.
## 2.1.3. Mixed metal sites
Mixed metal compounds including oxides, sulfides, phosphides, and hydroxides have been demonstrated to have enhanced performances for various energy conversion applications superior to their mono-metal compound counterparts due to the synergistic effect between the different metals.[73-79] MOFs with uniformly dispersed mixed metal sites can be facilely synthesized using mixed metal salts as precursors and the synergistic effects between the different metal sites can greatly boost the energy conversion performance of MOFs. For example, Wang and co-workers reported that a Ni/Fe bimetallic MOF, Ni/Fe-BTC (BTC= 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid), showed much enhanced electrocatalytic OER performance than the monometallic MOF counterparts in terms of overpotentials at 10 mA $\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ and Tafel  slopes.[80] In 2016, the synergistic effects between the mixed metal sites of MOFs that boosted the electrocatalytic OER performance were investigated both experimentally and theoretically by Zhao and co-workers (Figure 4A-H).[81] Using ${\mathsf{N i}}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{C o}^{2+}$ , and benzenedicarboxylic acid (BDC) as precursors, they successfully synthesized ultrathin NiCobimetal-organic frameworks nanosheets (NiCo-UMOFNs) with thickness of ${\sim}3.1$ nm which corresponded to four metal coordination layers or three coordination structural layers. The ultrathin nanosheet morphology of NiCo-UMOFNs led to abundant coordinatively unsaturated metal sites on the exposed surface because of the partially terminated BDC coordination bonding, which were demonstrated by X-ray absorption spectra (XAS) spectroscopy and corresponding calculations. By virtue of the unsaturated mixed Ni/Co sites, NiCo-UMOFNs exhibited much better electrocatalytic OER performance than the monometallic counterparts Co-UMOFNs and Ni-UMOFNs as well as the bulk NiCo-MOFs in 1 M KOH (Figure 4E).  The enhanced performance of NiCo-UMOFNs over bulk NiCo-MOFs was studied using in situ XAS spectra, which showed that the unsaturated Ni and Co sites in NiCo-UMOFNs are more readily oxidized and thus more active in the range of electrocatalytic OER potential from $\boldsymbol{1.43\vee}$ to $\boldsymbol{1.53\vee}$ compared with bulk NiCo-MOFs (Figure 4F-G). Also, partial density of states (PDOS) calculations showed that the $3d~\boldsymbol{e}_{9}$ states of the unsaturated metal sites (especially the unsaturated Ni sites) of NiCo-UMOFNs were more filled than the saturated metal sites of bulk NiCo-MOFs, leading to more active interactions between these metal sites and OER intermediates and thus enhanced electrocatalytic activity. The synergistic effect of Ni and Co sites that led to much improved OER performance of NiCo-UMOFNs than the monometallic counterparts Co-UMOFNs and Ni-UMOFNs was also investigated by DFT calculations. It was shown that the electron is partially transferred from $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ through the oxygen atoms of the organic ligands, leading to optimized filling of the $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{e}}_{9}$ -orbital of the unsaturated metal sites and improved OER performance (Figure 4H).
Zhao and co-workers reported ultrathin MOFs nanosheets with mixed metal sites (NiFe-MOF) grown on nickel foam for electrocatalytic OER and HER (Figure 5A-G).[82] The utilization of nickel foam as substrate can provide macropores for facile mass transport and high electrically conductive networks for promoted electron transfer, while the ultrathin 2D morphology endowed the MOF array grown on the surface of nickel foam with abundant exposed mixed metal sites. As a result, it showed excellent electrocatalytic OER and HER performance with overpotential at $10\ m A\ \mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ of $240~\mathsf{m V}$ (OER) and $134~\mathsf{m V}$ (HER), along with remarkable durability without significant activity loss during continuous operation for 20000 s (OER) and 2000 s (HER) in 0.1 M KOH. Compared with mono-metallic Ni-MOF, it was demonstrated that the incorporation of Fe to afford mixed metallic NiFe-MOF can introduce additional structural vacancies which acted as shallow donors to increase the carrier concentration of the metal octahedral units and thus facilitated the charge transfer during electrocatalysis, leading to much improved OER and HER performance than the monometallic counterparts. The aforementioned two cases reveal the significance of the synergistic effect between the different metal sites and the 2D morphology engineering that can lead to abundant exposed metal sites of MOFs for boosting the catalytic performances for energy conversion applications.
## 2.2. ADMSs in organic ligands of pristine MOFs
Besides metal nodes, ADMSs can be also incorporated in organic ligands of pristine MOFs using special ligands containing heteroatoms with lone pair electrons (normally nitrogen) that can coordinate with the metal sites (e.g. porphyrin-based and bipyridine-based ligands). By using such ligands, homogeneous metal complexes that have been well-studied and widely employed in various energy conversion applications can be uniformly anchored in the ligands of MOFs to afford heterogeneous catalysts, bridging the gap between heterogeneous and homogeneous catalysts (Figure 6). Incorporation of the molecular metal sites into the ligands of MOFs can offer many merits (Figure 6):
(1) More than one kind of metal complexes or other active components (e.g. Pt nanomaterials and polyoxometalates) can be incorporated into one MOF crystal to afford multi-functional catalysts where the intimate contact and synergistic effect between these incorporated components can greatly enhance the energy conversion performances (especially photocatalytic applications).
(2) The strong coordination bonding between the nitrogen atoms of the ligands and the metal centers can tightly immobilize the ADMSs, suppressing the degradation of the metal sites through the multi-molecule pathway.
(3) Incorporation of the homogeneous metal sites into the ligands of MOFs can afford heterogeneous catalysts that are more facile to be separated from the reaction solution and reused for another reaction cycle.
Various metal sites have been successfully incorporated in the organic ligands of MOFs including earth-abundant metal sites like Co, Fe, and Mn and precious metal sites like Pt, Re, Ir, and Ru, which will be discussed in the following sections.
## Homogeneous toHeterogeneous
## 2.2.1. Co sites
Homogeneous cobalt-metalated porphyrin-based molecules have been reported as efficient and selective electrocatalysts for $C O_{2}R R$ .[89-91] Using porphyrin-based ligands as linkers, the active cobalt-metalated porphyrin units can be uniformly incorporated into MOFs to afford heterogeneous electrocatalysts for $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ . For example, Kornienko and co-workers successfully grew a cobalt-porphyrin MOF, $A l_{2}(O H)_{2}$ TCPP-Co (TCP $\therefore H_{2}=4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime},4^{\prime\prime}-$ (porphyrin-5,10,15,20-tetrayl)tetrabenzoate), on a conductive substrate, which showed desirable electrocatalytic $C O_{2}R R$ to CO performance with turnover number (TON) of 1400 and selectivity of $76\%$ along with high durability over 7 days (Figure 7A-F).[92] $I n$ situ spectroelectrochemical analysis demonstrated that the Co(II) sites in the porphyrin units of $\mathsf{A l}_{2}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}\mathsf{T C P P-C o}$ were reduced to the catalytically active $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{I})$ sites that are supposed to adsorb a $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecule and reduce it into CO at potential range of $C O_{2}R R$ (Figure 7D-F). Besides electrocatalytic $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ catalysis, MOFs constructed with cobalt-porphyrin-based ligands also show promise for photocatalytic $C O_{2}R R$ by virtue of the incorporated photosensitive porphyrin units. Zhang and co-workers successfully incorporated Co sites in the porphyrin units of MOF525 $(Z\mathsf{r}_{6}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}({\mathsf{O H}})_{4}({\mathsf{T C P P-H}}_{2})_{3})$ constructed with $Z\mathsf{r}_{6}$ clusters and porphyrin-based ligands, which was employed in photocatalytic $C O_{2}R R$ (Figure 7G-M).[93] The resultant MOF-525-Co exhibited photo-absorption in the UV-Vis range from 200 to $800~\mathsf{n m}$ . In photocatalytic system with triethanolamine (TEOA) as electron donor, MOF-525-Co can efficiently reduce $\mathsf{C O}_{2}^{\prime}$ to CO and $C H_{4}$ with rates of 200.6 and $36.76\upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathfrak{g}^{-1}\ \mathsf{h}^{-1}$ , respectively, which are much higher than those of MOF-525 without Co incorporation and MOF-525-Zn incorporated with Zn, indicating the essential role of the Co sites for photocatalytic $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ . In situ Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra revealed strong interaction between the Co sites and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecules, and the photoluminescent (PL) quenching experiments demonstrated that after incorporation of the Co sites the separation of photo-excited electron-hole pairs was significantly facilitated while the electron-hole recombination was greatly suppressed due to the directional electron migration from porphyrin units to the incorporated Co sites. The efficient charge separation can provide abundant long-lived electrons to the Co sites, where the reduction of the adsorbed $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecules to Co and $C H_{4}$ takes place.
## 2.2.2. Fe sites
In additional to Co, Fe is another metal site that can be incorporated into the porphyrin units in MOFs constructed with porphyrin-based ligands for energy conversion applications. For instance, Hod and co-workers successfully loaded a thin film of Fe-metalated MOF-525 (MOF-525-Fe) on fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) glass substrate with thickness of several hundreds of nanometers, which is equivalent to $\mathord{\sim}900$ monolayers of surfaceadsorbed molecular Fe-TPP.[94] This MOF-525-Fe thin film showed an electrocatalytic $C O_{2}R R$ performance in 1 M ${\mathsf{T B A P F}}_{6}$ acetonitrile solution with TON and turnover frequency (TOF) of
CO production over ${\sim}4\ h$ of 272 and $64~\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ , respectively, along with Faradaic efficiency of $\sim100\%$ (CO and ${\sf H}_{2}$ ). The desirable electrocatalytic $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ performance was attributed to the abundant catalytically active Fe-porphyrin sites, well-defined nanoscale porosity of MOF-525-Fe for facile access of the reactants to the Fe-porphyrin sites, and the fast charge transfer by redox hopping between neighboring Fe-porphyrin sites. The CO production rate of MOF-525-Fe was further improved by addition of 2,2,2-trifluoroethanol (TFE) that can facilitate the C-O bond cleavage and desorption of the CO molecules, leading to CO TON and TOF over $3.2\mathsf{h}$ of 1520 and $0.13\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ .
Biomimetic [FeFe] complexes with $\mathsf{F e}_{2}$ active sites similar to [FeFe] hydrogenases are well-known efficient molecular catalysts for hydrogen production.[95-97] Through a postsynthetic exchange method, Pullen and co-workers successfully incorporated [FeFe] sites into the ligands of a highly robust Zr-based MOF (UiO-66) using $[{\mathsf{F e F e}}]({\mathsf{d c b d t}})({\mathsf{C O}})_{6}$ (dcbdt $^{=1,4}$ -dicarboxylbenzene-2,3- ditiolate) as linker to afford a heterogeneous catalyst (UiO-66- [FeFe](dcbdt)- $({\mathsf{C O}})_{6})$ with high activity and recyclability (Figure 8A-C).[98] In photocatalytic system involving $[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}]^{2+}$ as photosensitizer and ascorbate as electron donor in $1.0\mathsf{M}$ acetate buffer solution $(p H=5)$ , the resultant heterogeneous UiO-66- [FeFe](dcbdt)- $({\mathsf{C O}})_{6}$ showed much enhanced photocatalytic hydrogen production performance over the homogeneous reference system with similar $\mathsf{F e}_{2}$ complex concentration in terms of initial hydrogen production rate and total obtained ${\sf H}_{2}$ amount. The improved photocatalytic hydrogen production performance of UiO-66-[FeFe](dcbdt)- $({\mathsf{C O}})_{6}$ over the homogeneous counterparts was attributed to the incorporation of abundant [FeFe] sites in the ligands of robust MOF that not only protected the reduced species from nonproductive charge recombination but also facilitated the disproportionation reactions to produce the catalytically active dianionic $\mathsf{F e}_{2}$ species.
In the case of UiO-66-[FeFe](dcbdt)- $({\mathsf{C O}})_{6}$ , it was suggested that only the [FeFe] sites located within several nanometers from the surface of the MOF crystals are viable catalytic sites as the rate of electron transfer from photogenerated $[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}]^{+}$ decreases exponentially with distance. A more intimate contact between the catalytically active Fe sites and photosensitive components is more desirable. Considering that the porphyrin units are well-known visible light absorbers with extremely large extinction coefficients[1, 99-100] and the metal sites in the porphyrin units can also act as “anchors” to immobilize the [FeFe] complexes through coordination bonds, Sasan and co-workers successfully coupled biomimetic $[\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{2}]$ complexes with the Znporphyrin units in the zirconium-porphyrin MOF (ZrPF), leading to intimate contact between the [FeFe] sites with catalytic activity and porphyrin units with photoresponse (Figure 8D).[101] The direct coupling of [FeFe] sites and porphyrin units can greatly facilitate the electron transfer and make full use of the [FeFe] sites inside the MOF crystals. In photocatalytic hydrogen production system with ascorbic acid as sacrificial electron donor in $1.0\mathrm{~M~}$ acetate buffer aqueous solution $(p H=5)$ under visible light irradiation, the resultant heterogeneous photocatalyst [FeFe]@ZrPF showed higher activity with hydrogen production of ${\sim}3.5~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}$ for 2 h and better durability than the homogeneous reference system, indicating the superior of the intimate coupling of the catalytically active [FeFe] sites and the photosensitive porphyrin units in the linkers of MOFs.
## 2.2.3. Other earth-abundant metal sites
In addition to Co and Fe, many other earth-abundant metal sites have also been successfully incorporated into the ligands of MOFs for energy conversion. Using a postsynthetic modification method, Fei and co-workers incorporated Mn bipyridine complex, $\mathsf{M n}(\mathsf{b p y d c})\cdot(\mathsf{C O})_{3}\mathsf{B r}$ (bpydc $\!=5,5^{\prime}$ -dicarboxylate-2,2′-bipyridine) with similar Mn site to the molecular f $a c-M n(b p y)(C O)_{3}B r$ counterparts well-known as efficient catalyst for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction, into the ligands of a Zr-based MOF (UiO-67) and employed it for photocatalysis of $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ (Figure 8E-F).[102] In photocatalytic system using $[\mathsf{R u(d m b)}_{3}]^{2+}$ ( $\mathsf{d m b}{=}4,4^{\prime}$ -dimethyl-2,2′-bipyridine) as photosensitizer and 1-benzyl-1,4-dihydronicotinamide (BNAH) as sacrificial reducing agent, the resultant heterogeneous UiO-67- $\mathsf{M n}(\mathsf{b p y})(\mathsf{C O})_{3}\mathsf{B r}$ catalyst showed desirable photocatalytic performance for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction to formate with TON of 110 over $18\mathsf{h}$ and selectivity of $\sim96\%$ as well as good recyclability, which exceeded the homogeneous reference systems. The improved activity of the Mn sites incorporated in MOF was attributed to the robust framework structure of the UiO-67 anchoring and isolating the bpy metal-chelating sites can efficiently prevent the dimerization of the single reduced Mn site.
In another case, Cr and Ga sites were incorporated into the ligands of UiO-66 through a postsynthetic exchange method to afford heterogeneous catalysts with isolated metalmonocatecholato moieties by Lee and co-workers.[103] In photocatalytic system with BNAH as sacrificial reducing agent and TEOA as sacrificial base, the resultant Cr- and Gamonocatecholato functionalized UiO-66 can selectively reduce $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to HCOOH with TON of ${\sim}11$ and ${\sim}6$ respectively over 6 h under visible light irradiation.
## 2.2.4. Ir sites
In addition to earth-abundant metal sites, incorporation of precious metal complexes into the organic ligands was also successfully achieved by many researchers. Indeed, homogeneous metal complexes based on precious metal such as Ir, Pt, Ru, and Re have been well-studied as photocatalysts for water oxidation, hydrogen production, and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction and as photosensitizers for light harvesting. Homogeneous precious metal complexes with catalytic or photosensitive activities can be incorporated in the linkers of MOFs using special linkers containing bpy or ppy (2-phenylpyridine) units to afford heterogeneous catalysts, as many of the precious metal complexes have bpy or ppy as ligands.
[Ir(ppy)2(bpy)]+ and its derivatives are well-known photosensitizers for light harvesting in various photocatalytic systems.[104] Using ligands with bpy units that are able to coordinate with Ir, the photosensitive $[\mathsf{I r}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]^{+}$ units can be incorporated into the linkers of MOFs. The incorporated $[\mathsf{I r}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]^{+}$ units can have intimate contact with other catalytically active components incorporated into the pores of MOFs, which can greatly facilitate the charge transfer from the photo-excited Ir sites to the catalytically active components and suppress the decomposition of the Ir sites. For example, Wang and co-workers incorporated the $[\mathsf{I r}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]^{+}$ units into the robust UiO-67 MOF using a $[\mathsf{I r}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]^{+}$ -derived dicarboxylate ligand (Figure 9A-B).[105] Pt nanomaterials, which act as catalytic sites for hydrogen production, were then introduced in the MOF by in situ photoreduction of ${\mathsf{K}}_{2}{\mathsf{P t C l}}_{4}$ making use of the photosensitive Ir sites. In photocatalytic hydrogen production system involving TEA as electron donor under visible light irradiation, the resultant MOF with Ir sites and Pt nanomaterials showed hydrogen production performance with Ir-TON of 7000 over $48\mathrm{~h~}$ , much higher than that of the homogeneous reference system. The enhanced photocatalytic performance was attributed to more facile electron transfer from the photosensitive Ir sites to catalytically active Pt nanomaterials.
Besides Pt nanoparticles, polyoxometalates (POMs) with hydrogen production catalytic activity were also introduced into MOFs incorporated with photosensitive Ir sites by Kong and coworkers (Figure 9C-G).[106] Ni-containing POM $[\mathsf{N i}_{4}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{2}(\mathsf{P W}_{9}\mathsf{O}_{34})_{2}]^{10-}\ (\mathsf{N i}_{4}\mathsf{P}_{2})$ was uniformly incorporated into the UiO MOF with abundant $[\mathsf{I r}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]^{+}$ units in ligands to afford heterogeneous photocatalysts where the catalyst POM have intimate contact with the photosensitizer Ir site. The resultant ${\mathsf{N i}}_{4}{\mathsf{P}}_{2}@{\mathsf{M O F}}$ showed much higher photocatalytic HER activity with TON of 1476 over $72~\mathsf{h}$ than that of the homogeneous counterparts, which was ascribed to the proximity of the catalyst Ni4P2 to multiple photosensitizers that facilitate multi-electron transfer from photo-excited $[\mathsf{I r}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]^{+}$ to $\mathsf{N i}_{4}\mathsf{P}_{2}$ .
In another case, $[\mathsf{I r}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]^{+}$ units were introduced into the ligands of a Y-based MOF, which exhibited photocatalytic $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction activity to produce HCOO- under visible light irradiation.[107]
## 2.2.5. Pt sites
Molecular $\mathsf{P t}^{\parallel}$ complexes have been widely investigated as efficient homogeneous photocatalysts for hydrogen production.[108] Similar to the cases of Ir sites, Pt complexes can be incorporated into the ligands of MOFs using linkers with bpy units. For example, Zhou and co-workers introduced $\mathsf{P t}(\mathsf{b p y})\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ moieties into the bpydc ligands of a Al-based MOF (Al(OH)(bpydc), MOF-253) through a postsynthetic modification method (Figure 10A-B).[109] The resultant MOF-253-Pt can act as both photosensitizer and photocatalyst for hydrogen production under visible light irradiation, showing higher activity and durability than the homogeneous counterpart. It was suggested that upon visible light irradiation, synergistic effect between two neighboring $\mathsf{P t}(\mathsf{b p y})\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ sites can lead to the formation of hydridediplatinum (II, III) intermediate which efficiently produce ${\sf H}_{2}$ through a heterolytic coupling pathway (Figure 10B).
In 2016, Kim and co-workers introduced both the aforementioned photosensitive Ir-based complexes and catalytically active Pt-based complexes into one UiO MOF crystal with abundant bpy sites in the ligands (Figure 10C-D).[110] In photocatalytic hydrogen production system using N, Ndimethylaniline (DMA) as sacrificial reducing agent under visible light irradiation, the resultant UiO MOF with both Ir and Pt sites showed high durability with negligible activity loss after continuous operation for 6.5 days, while the homogeneous reference system suffered from severe decomposition of the molecular complexes to colloids. The extremely high durability was ascribed to the abundant uniformly distributed diimine sites throughout the whole MOF crystal that can facilitate the rebonding of the broken metal−diimine coordination bonds during photocatalytic hydrogen production operation.
## 2.2.6. Ru sites
Besides $[\mathsf{I r}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]^{+}$ , $[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}]^{2+}$ with strong and broad absorbance of visible light and long excited state lifetime is another commonly used molecular photosensitizer in various photocatalysis systems, which can also be incorporated into the ligands of MOFs using linkers with bpy sites.[111]  For example, Hou and co-workers incorporated both $[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}]^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{P t}(\mathsf{b p y})\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ sites into the UiO-67 MOF using a mix-and-match approach (Figure 11A-B).[112] The resultant di-component MOF showed much enhanced photocatalytic hydrogen production performance compared with homogeneous counterparts under visible light irradiation, which was attributed to the short distance between the photosensitizer Ru sites and catalyst $\mathsf{P t}$ sites that facilitated the electron transfer. Photosensitive Ru sites were also combined with catalytically active POMs in MOF by Zhang and co-workers.[113] Through a one-pot self-assembly process, a Wells-Dawson-type POM $[\mathsf{P}_{2}\mathsf{W}_{18}\mathsf{O}_{62}]^{6-}$ was introduced to the cavities of UiO MOF incorporated with $[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p}\mathsf{y})_{3}]^{2+}$ units in the ligands (Figure 11C-D). In photocatalytic hydrogen production system using methanol as sacrificial electron donor in acidic solution $(p H=1.8)$ ), the resultant POM@UiO delivered a maximum TON of 79 over $14\ h$ along with excellent recyclability with negligible activity loss after at least 3 cycles. Introduction of the POMs in the cavities of the UiO MOF leaded to facile multielectron injection from the photoexcited $[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p}\mathsf{y})_{3}]^{2+}$ units to POM, which was surrounded by six or twelve $[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p}\mathsf{y})_{3}]^{2+}$ -based ligands in tetrahedral or octahedral cages, respectively (Figure 11D).
Besides serving as photosensitizers in the aforementioned cases, Ru-complexes incorporated into the ligands of MOFs can also act as catalyst sites for energy conversion. For example, a RuII-CO complex that is photocatalytically active for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction, $[\mathsf{R u}^{\mathsf{u}}(\mathsf{b p y})(\mathsf{t e r p y})(\mathsf{C O})]^{2+}$ $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{terpy}}=2,2^{\prime}:6^{\prime},2"$ -terpyridine), was introduced to UiO-67 MOF using a postsynthetic exchange method by Kajiwara and co-workers (Figure 11E-F).[114] The resultant $\mathsf{P C P-R u}^{\mathsf{I I}}$ was found to be an excellent $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorbent with selective adsorption towards $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ from other gas like ${\sf N}_{2}$ and Ar as well as an efficient ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction catalyst, which showed high photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction performance  even at low ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ concentration $(5\%$ $,\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{A r})$ due to the synergistic effect $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption sites and catalytically active sites.
In another case, $\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})(\mathsf{C O})_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ sites with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction photocatalytic activity were introduced into the ligands of a Albased MOF, which showed $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ photocatalytic performance with TON for the production of HCOO- and CO of 2.9 and 7.1 respectively over $^{8\mathfrak{h}}$ under visible light irradiation.[115]
## 2.2.7. Re sites
Re-based complexes have been well-studied as photocatalysts for $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ .[116-119] Using ligands with bpy sites as building linkers, Re sites can be uniformly introduced into the linkers of MOFs to afford heterogeneous photocatalysts for efficient $C O_{2}R R$ . Huang and co-workers successfully incorporated $\mathsf{R e}(\mathsf{b p y})(\mathsf{C O})_{3}\mathsf{C l}$ complex, a well-known molecular photocatalyst for selective ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction to CO, into the linkers of a highly stable and robust UiO MOF using elongated dicarboxylate modified $\mathsf{R e}(\mathsf{b p y})(\mathsf{C O})_{3}\mathsf{C l}$ units as linkers.[120] In $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ -saturated acetonitrile with TEA as sacrificial reducing agent, the resultant Re-modified UiO MOF showed photocatalytic performance towards selective $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to CO with TON for CO of 6.44 over 6 h, much higher than that of the homogeneous counterpart.
The photocatalytic $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction performance of Refunctionalized MOF can be further improved by introduction of plasmonic metal nanomaterials. Choi and co-workers synthesized $A g C R e_{3}$ -MOF (3 Re complexes per unit cell) core-shell composite with Ag nanocube as core and $\mathsf{R e}(\mathsf{b p y})(\mathsf{C O})_{3}\mathsf{C l}$ -modified MOF as shell for plasmon-enhanced photocatalytic $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction to CO (Figure 12A-C).[121] Upon light irradiation, the Re sites coated on the Ag nanocubes were subjected to the greatly intensified nearsurface electric field surface at surface plasmon resonance frequency produced on the surface of the Ag nanocubes, leading to a 7-fold increasement of CO TON compared with Refunctionalized MOF without Ag nanocubes.
In another case, $\mathsf{R e}(\mathsf{b p y})(\mathsf{C O})_{3}\mathsf{C l}$ -functionalized MOF thin film was deposited onto a conductive FTO substrate through liquid-phase epitaxy by Ye and co-workers, which showed high faradaic efficiency of ${\sim}93\%$ for electrocatalytic $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction to CO.[122]
## 2.3. ADMSs in pores of pristine MOFs
In addition to metal nodes and organic ligands, ADMSs can be also incorporated into the pores of MOFs. The well-defined pores of MOFs with suitable pore sizes can efficiently encage and isolate the metal complexes to afford heterogeneous catalysts with ADMSs incorporated within the pores. Compared with the homogeneous metal complex counterparts, the metal complexes are isolated from each other, and therefore the degradation and de-activation of the complexes induced by interactions between more than one molecule are prevented, leading to much enhanced durability.
A high-valent di- $\upmu$ -oxo dimanganese complex ([(terpy)Mn(μ- $\mathsf{O})_{2}\mathsf{M n}](\mathsf{t e r p y})\mathsf{l}^{3+})$ , MnTD, with high activity for water oxidation were incorporated into the cage-like pores of a Cr-based MOF MIL-101(Cr) by Nepal and co-workers (Figure 13A-B).[123] Despite high water oxidation activity, homogeneous MnTD suffers from severe degradation caused by intermolecular interactions between more than one MnTD molecules. The well-defined cagelike cages with small windows of MIL-101(Cr) can efficiently encage and isolate the MnTD from each other, preventing the degradation of MnTD involving multiple molecules. As a result, MnTD encapsulated in MIL-101(Cr) showed water oxidation catalytic activity comparable to homogeneous MnTD and remarkably improved durability.
Using MOFs with photoresponse to confine catalytically active metal sites in the pores can construct heterogeneous photocatalysts with facile charge transfer from the photosensitive units to catalytic sites. For instance, Nasalevich and co-workers encapsulated the cobaloxime complexes with catalytic activity for hydrogen production into the pores of a Ti-based MOF $N H_{2}$ -MIL125(Ti)) with visible light photoresponse using a “Ship-in-a-bottle” strategy (Figure 13C-F).[124] The resultant heterogeneous Co@MOF showed remarkably enhanced photocatalytic hydrogen production performance compared with homogeneous cobaloxime complexes and pristine $N H_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) with a steady ${\sf H}_{2}$ TOF of $0.8~\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ over $65{\textrm{h}}$ operation under visible light irradiation, along with excellent recyclability for at least 3 times without activity loss. It was proposed that upon light irradiation, the photosensitive linkers of $N H_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) can absorb photon and rapidly inject electrons to the Co sites within the pores to reduce the ${\mathsf{C o}}^{\parallel\parallel}$ to $\mathsf{C o}^{\mathsf{I I}}$ , which then efficiently catalyzed the hydrogen production reaction (Figure 13D).
Besides Mn and Co sites, Ni sites were also introduced within the pores of MOFs for energy conversion. An and co-workers incorporated ${\mathsf{N i}}^{2+}$ cations into the pores of an Al-based MOF (AlATA) through the coordination interactions with the amino groups and ${\mathsf{A l O}}_{6}$ octahedra of Al-ATA.[125] After incorporation of ${\mathsf{N i}}^{2+}$ cations, the resultant Al-ATA-Ni showed much enhanced photocatalytic overall water splitting performance compared with pristine Al-ATA: $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ and ${\sf H}_{2}$ evolution rates of Al-ATA-Ni were 155 and $36~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ respectively, while the pristine Al-ATA was inactive for ${\sf H}_{2}$ production and delivered an $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ evolution rate of only $16.5\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathsf{h}^{-1}$ . The incorporation of ${\mathsf{N i}}^{2+}$ in the pores greatly facilitated the electron-hole separation, leading to much more free photogenerated charge carriers for photocatalytic water splitting. It was proposed that upon light irradiation, photogenerated electrons rapidly transferred from the linkers to the ${\mathsf{N i}}^{2+}$ sites in the pores to produce catalytically active ${\mathsf{N i^{+}}}$ sites for catalysis of ${\sf H}_{2}$ production, while the photogenerated holes stayed in the linkers and catalyzed the $\O_{2}$ evolution reaction.
## 3. ADMSs in MOF-derived materials
MOFs serve as ideal precursors to synthesize porous carbon materials doped with ADMSs, as MOFs possess abundant uniformly distributed organic ligands serving as carbon sources and ADMSs in the metal nodes, organic linkers, and pores as discussed in the previous section (Figure 14). Most of the pristine
MOFs suffer from poor chemical and thermal stabilities, limiting their applications in harsh conditions. In contrast, in MOF-derived carbon materials, ADMSs are normally coordinated with 2 to 4 doped nitrogen atoms through strong metal-nitrogen coordination bonds, which together with the stable carbon networks leads to largely improved chemical and thermal stability of the ADMSs doped MOF-derived carbon materials. The utilization of MOFs as templates to derive ADMSs in carbon materials can exhibit many merits:
(1) The MOF-derived carbon materials can to some extent inherit the high surface area and porosity of MOF precursors, which leads to sufficient exposure and accessibility of the active ADMSs as well as facilitated mass transport.
(2) Various metal sites including precious and non-precious metal can be facilely and uniformly introduce in the metal nodes, ligands, and pores of the MOF precursors, which can be transferred to various ADMSs with different catalytic behaviors in MOF-derived carbon materials for specific energy conversion applications. Also, more than one kind of metal sites can be simultaneously introduced into one MOF precursor crystal to afford MOF-derived carbon materials with mixed ADMSs where the synergistic effect between the different ADMSs can greatly promote the catalytic activity or offer unique physical and chemical properties.
(3) The easily tunable material sizes and shapes of the MOF precursors offer possibility to tailor and control the morphologies of the MOF-derived carbon for more sufficient exposure and making full use of the ADMSs.
(4) Abundant nitrogen is presented in MOF precursors constructed with nitrogen-containing ligands, which can be transferred to nitrogen atoms doped in carbon materials that can efficiently anchor and stabilize the ADMSs during high temperature pyrolysis.
(5) The transformation from pristine MOFs to carbon materials results in greatly improved electrical conductivity and thermal/chemical stability, making ADMSs in MOF-derived carbon more promising for energy conversion applications (especially electrocatalytic applications).
Despite the aforementioned advantages, the high temperature pyrolysis of the MOF precursors always led to aggregated metal materials rather than ADMSs in most of the reported works due to the high surface energy of the single metal sites. Acid treatment was then necessary to remove the aggregated metal materials and leave the ADMSs in MOFderived carbon materials. However, some of the metal materials were wrapped and protected by graphitic carbon shells, which required more corrosive acids and more harsh acid treatment conditions in order to completely remove these metal materials. Another method to transfer MOFs into ADMSs is utilization of Znbased MOFs doped with target metal sites as precursors. The utilization of Zn can greatly lower the concentration and increase the adjacent distance of the target metal sites in the MOF crystals, which are unlikely to aggregate at elevated temperature. During high temperature pyrolysis, Zn can be easily evaporated as the boiling point of Zn is only $907^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , leaving porous carbon networks uniformly doped with the target metal sites.[126] Nitrogencontaining Zn-based MOFs are preferred as nitrogen can efficiently anchor and stabilize the ADMSs, suppressing the migration and aggregation of the metal atoms. ZIF-8 constructed with Zn cations as metal nodes and 2-methylimidazolate as organic linkers represents an ideal platform to achieve ADMSs in MOF-derived carbon materials through one-step pyrolysis without acid treatment. The target metal sites can be facilely introduced into the pores and metal nodes of ZIF-8 with low concentration and expanded spatial distance, which suppresses the aggregation of the target metal atoms during high temperature pyrolysis. Moreover, the abundant nitrogen atoms presented in the 2-methylimidazolate ligands can serve as anchoring sites to fix and stabilize the ADMSs. Utilization of ZIF-8 also has the advantages that nanosized ZIF-8 crystals with regular morphology and tunable material sizes are facile to synthesize, which can be well maintained after heat treatment to afford ADMSs in well-defined nanosized carbon materials, leading to sufficient exposure of the active ADMSs and facile mass transport.
Compared with metal materials/clusters, ADMSs in MOFderived carbon materials have many advantages for energy conversion applications:
(1) Reducing the metal material size to the utmost to afford ADMSs can make full use of every metal sites (i.e. $100\%$ metal atom utilization), leading to high catalytic activity. (2) The highly coordinatively unsaturated nature of the ADMSs provides a large number of open sites for adsorption and activation of the reactant molecules. (3) The well-defined configuration of ADMSs represents an ideal model to investigate and reveal the catalytic mechanism and a desirable platform to design and construct catalytically active metal sites with unique electro-/photo-chemical properties at atomic level.
Table 2 summarized typical ADMS-incorporated MOFderived carbon materials for energy conversion catalysis.
## 3.1. Co sites in MOF-derived materials
$C O-N_{x}-C$ sites have been widely accepted as active sites for electrocatalysis of ORR.[23, 133-134] MOFs constructed with Co nodes and nitrogen-containing ligands represent highly promising precursors to synthesize Co- $\cdot\mathsf{N}_{\mathsf{x}}$ -C electrocatalysts because of the uniformly distributed Co and N throughout the whole MOF crystals, high surface area and porosity, and presence of abundant Co-N coordination bonds, which can be to some extent inherited after high temperature pyrolysis, leading to sufficiently exposed Co- ${\bf\cdot N_{x}}$ - C sites uniformly distributed throughout the porous MOF-derived carbon materials.[31-32, 135-136] However, it was found that simple heat treatment of Co-based MOFs tended to result in aggregated Co nanomaterials driven by surface energy rather than atomically dispersed $C o-N_{x}-C$ sites. The presence of these Co nanomaterials makes the active sites of the resultant electrocatalysts elusive, or even blocks the $\mathsf{C o-N_{x}}$ -C sites. Acid leaching treatments were then indispensable to remove the metallic Co materials. However, the metallic Co materials were protected by graphitic carbon shells formed at high temperature with the catalysis of Co, so that rigorous and tedious acid treatments using highly corrosive acids (e.g. HF) are necessary to completely remove these protected Co nanomaterials. It is desirable that ADMSs in carbon materials without aggregated Co nanomaterials can be directly obtained after heat treatment so that the utilization of corrosive and toxic acids for acid treatment can be avoided. The key is to reduce the concentration and increase the spatial distance of the target Co sites, and in the meanwhile provides anchoring sites to stabilize the single Co sites. As mentioned above, ZIF-8 constructed with $Z n$ cations as metal nodes and 2-methylimidazolate as organic linkers represents an ideal platform to achieve ADMSs in MOF-derived carbon materials through one-step pyrolysis without acid treatment. In 2016, Yin and co-workers successfully synthesized atomically dispersed $\mathsf{C o-N_{x}}$ sites in porous carbon materials through onestep pyrolysis of a bimetallic $Z n/C o$ ZIF with homogeneous distributed $Z n^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{C o}^{2+}$ (molar ratio $\scriptstyle1=1:1$ ) without further acid treatment (Figure 15A-H).[137] Compared with monometallic Cobased ZIF, the bimetallic $Z n/C o$ ZIF had lower ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ concentration and expanded spatial distance of adjacent ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ that can suppress the aggregation of the Co sites at elevated temperature, as $Z n^{2+}$ replaced half of the ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ in the metal nodes. Upon high temperature pyrolysis, the $z_{n}$ cations were reduced to metallic Zn with low boiling point, which was then evaporated away and left a large amount of free N sites for stabilization of the single Co sites. XAFS measurements revealed that the configurations of the atomically dispersed $\mathsf{C o-N_{x}-C}$ can be precisely tuned by adjusting the pyrolytic temperature. $C o-N_{4}-C$ was the major configuration of the single Co sites after heat treatment at $800^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ while Co- ${\sf N}_{2}$ -C sites with were obtained at a higher pyrolytic temperature, $900^{\circ}\mathsf{C},$ which was attributed to the breaks of certain Co-N bonds at elevated temperature (Figure 15F-G). In alkaline solution, the resultant atomically dispersed ${\mathsf{C o-N}}_{2^{-}}{\mathsf{C}}$ in carbon materials obtained at $900~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ showed excellent ORR electrocatalytic performance with onset potential of $0.982\mathrm{~V~}$ and half-wave potential of $0.881\mathrm{~V~}$ , better than those of the $C o-N_{4}-C$ catalysts obtained at $800~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and the commercial $\mathsf{P t/C}$ catalyst. DFT calculations revealed that ${\mathsf{C o-N}}_{2}{\mathsf{-C}}$ sites exhibited stronger interaction with peroxide intermediate than $C o-N_{4}-C$ sites and a promoted 4-electron pathway for efficient ORR, which helped explain the superior electrocatalytic performance of the ${\mathsf{C o-N}}_{2^{-}}{\mathsf{C}}$ catalysts. The single $C O-N_{2}-C$ sites doped carbon catalysts also had excellent stability during long-term operation, which can remain intact after at least 5000 continuous cycles.
Ahn and co-workers used a bimetallic $Z n/C o$ ZIF as template to synthesize atomically dispersed Co- $\cdot N_{x^{-C}}$ sites decorated porous carbon materials (Figure 15I-K).[138] In this case, another template, tellurium nanotube, was additionally used to afford 1D structure that was supposed to promote the electron transfer and mass transport during ORR electrocatalysis. After high temperature pyrolysis, $C o-N_{x}-C$ sites atomically dispersed in 1D dual-porous carbon structure were obtained as the tubular tellurium cores and Zn were evaporated away. The resultant 1D carbon nanotubes with abundant $\mathsf{C o-N_{x}-C}$ showed desirable ORR performance with onset and half-wave potentials of 1.00 and 0.87 V respectively, along with excellent durability with less than $3\%$ current density loss after 24 h of chronoamperometry test.
## 3.2. Fe sites in MOF-derived materials
Besides $C o-N_{x}-C$ , Fe- $\cdot N_{x}-C$ catalysts were also widely investigated as highly active electrocatalysts for ORR.[133-134, 139- 142] Similar to the aforementioned $\mathsf{C o-N_{x}-}$ C derived from Co-doped ZIF-8, Fe-Nx-C can be produced using Fe-doped ZIF-8 as precursor. Chen and co-workers successfully synthesized atomically dispersed $F e\mathrm{-}N_{x}\mathrm{-}C$ sites in carbon materials through one-step pyrolysis of the ZIF-8 with ${\sf F e}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{3}$ uniformly incorporated into the pores (Figure 16A-E).[143] As revealed using XAFS measurements, the configuration of the atomically dispersed Fe site was Fe-N4-C with one $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ molecule perpendicularly adsorbed on Fe atom, which has been demonstrated an highly active site for electrocatalysis of ORR. In alkaline solution, the resultant MOF-derived carbon materials doped with atomically dispersed Fe- ${\cdot}{\mathsf{N}}_{4}$ -C sites showed excellent ORR performance with half-wave potential of $0.9\:\forall$ , 58 mV more positive than that of the state-of-the-art $\mathsf{P t/C}$ catalyst, along with superb durability with negligible change of the half-wave potential after 5000 cycles.
As mentioned above, the utilization of ZIF-8 as precursor offers the opportunity to construct carbon materials doped with single Fe sites with well-defined morphologies and tunable materials sizes, as well-defined ZIF-8 crystals of different material sizes can be facilely synthesized under different conditions. It is expected that more active single Fe sites can be exposed as the material size decreases, leading to better electrocatalytic performance. Zhang and co-workers systematically investigated the size effect of the MOF-derived carbon materials with single Fe sites on the ORR electrocatalytic performance (Figure 16F-J).[144] Fe-doped ZIF-8 of different material sizes from 20 to 1000 nm were synthesized and then used as precursors to produced single Fe sites doped carbon nanomaterials of different material sizes through one-step high temperature pyrolysis. It was found that the ORR performance was greatly dependent on the material sizes of the resultant carbon nanomaterials doped with atomically dispersed Fe sites: better ORR performances were exhibited as the material sized decreased from 1000 to $50\mathsf{n m}$ in terms of halfwave potentials and current densities at kinetic ranges $(j_{\mathrm{k}}{=}0.85\vee)$ . The size-dependent ORR activities were attributed to larger electrochemically accessible surface area and more accessible active single Fe sites in carbon nanomaterials of smaller material sizes. Further reducing the material size to $20~\mathsf{n m}$ , however, led to a decrease of accessible active sites and poorer ORR activity because of severe agglomeration of the $20~\mathsf{n m}$ nanomaterials. The effect of pyrolytic temperature on ORR performance was also investigated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis. It was found that more graphitic N and Fe- ${\cdot}{\mathsf{N}}_{4}$ -C sites were presented as the pyrolytic temperature increased, resulting in higher ORR activity. This research demonstrated the importance of high exposure and accessibility of the active Fe-Nx-C sites for advanced ORR electrocatalysis, which can be achieved by reducing the material size while avoiding agglomeration of the carbon materials.
Ye and co-workers presented another way to fabricate MOFderived carbon materials with highly exposed and accessible single Fe-Nx-C sites by selectively introducing the Fe- ${\bf\nabla\cdot N_{x}}$ -C sites on the surface of the carbon materials.[145] Fe source (ammonium ferric citrate) was selectively confined on the surface of ZIF-8 nanocrystals using a post-synthetic modification method. After high temperature pyrolysis, atomically dispersed Fe- $\cdot N_{x}-C$ sites were produced on the surface of the ZIF-derived carbon nanomaterials, which were sufficiently exposed and highly accessible to the reactant molecules. As a result of the improved accessibility of the active Fe-Nx-C sites on the surface, it showed electrocatalytic performance for ORR and $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ superior to the counterpart ZIF-derived carbon nanomaterials with Fe- ${\sf N}_{\sf x}$ -C sites located deeply inside the carbon nanomaterials.
Highly active Fe- $\cdot\mathsf{N}_{\mathsf{x}}$ -C catalysts suffered from severe current loss when employed for proton-exchange-membrane fuel cells (PEMFCs). The well-defined configuration of the atomically dispersed $F e\mathrm{-}N_{x}\mathrm{-}C$ sites in MOF-derived carbon nanomaterials offers a great opportunity to design and improve the durability of the $F e\mathrm{-}N_{x}\mathrm{-}C$ sites at atomic level. Zeng and co-workers successfully anchored one Pt atom onto each Fe-Nx-C site in carbon nanomaterials derived from Fe-incorporated ZIF-8 through a bridging $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ molecule using a “single-atom to singleatom” grafting method.[146] The Pt-modulated $F e\mathrm{-}N_{x}\mathrm{-}C$ sites displayed much enhanced ORR durability in acidic solution due to the protection of the grafted $\mathsf{P t}_{1}..\mathsf{O}_{2}$ - caps on the Fe- $\cdot N_{x}-C$ sites. Moreover, much improved electrocatalytic HER and OER performances were delivered by these novel $\mathsf{P t}_{1}..\mathsf{O}_{2^{-}}\mathsf{F e}_{1}..\mathsf{N}_{4}$ sites, which was attributed to the synergistic effects of the heteroatoms that can greatly promote the electron transfer during the proton reduction and desorption.
In other cases, Zitolo and co-workers demonstrated the existence of porphyrin-like $F e N_{4}C_{12}$ moieties in ZIF-derived carbon materials, which can efficiently catalyze ORR through the 4-electron pathway.[139] Ahn and co-workers synthesized 1D porous carbon nanotubes with atomically dispersed Fe- $\cdot N_{x}-C$ sites using tellurium nanotubes and ZIF-8 as templates for efficient ORR in both acidic and alkaline solutions.[147] Zhu and co-workers synthesized Fe- $\cdot N_{x}-C$ doped honeycomb-like porous carbons frameworks using MIL- $101\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ with dicyandiamide and ${\mathsf{F e C l}}_{3}$ in pores as precursors, which exhibited high ORR activity in alkaline solution.[148]
## 3.3. Ni sites in MOF-derived materials
In addition to Co and Fe, carbon materials with atomically dispersed Ni sites can be also produced using MOFs as precursors. Zhao and co-workers synthesized carbon nanomaterials with atomically dispersed Ni sites through one-step pyrolysis of the precursor Ni-incorporated ZIF-8 (Figure 17AB).[149] Ni source $({\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{2}$ aqueous solution) was uniformly incorporated into the pores of ZIF-8 (dispersed in n-hexane) through a double-solvent method. During high temperature pyrolysis, the Zn species were evaporated away and abundant free N sites were left, which served as anchoring sites for stabilization and isolation of the single Ni sites. XAFS measurements revealed the absence of aggregated metallic Ni materials and the existence of atomically dispersed Ni sites with 3 nitrogen atoms coordinated with each Ni center. The resultant ZIF-8-derived carbon nanomaterials doped with atomically dispersed Ni sites can efficiently and selectively reduce $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to
CO with desirable current density and Faradaic efficiency by virtue of the presence of abundant single Ni-N3-C sites with low CO adsorption energy and the carbon networks with high electrical conductivity.
Fan and co-workers explored a new strategy to afford atomically dispersed Ni sites from a Ni-based MOF (Figure 17CH).[150] Ni nanomaterials encapsulated in graphene layers $({\mathsf{N i}}@{\mathsf{C}})$ were first obtained after pyrolysis of  the Ni-based MOF at $700^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , which was then subjected to HCl leaching to remove most of the metallic Ni materials. The remaining Ni materials protected by graphitic layers can be transferred to atomically isolated Ni sites through electrochemical activation, which were demonstrated by X-ray diffraction (XRD), XPS and atomic resolution HAADF measurements. It was found that after electrochemical activations, the resultant single Ni sites showed significantly enhanced electrocatalytic performance towards HER with more positive onset potential, lower overpotential at $10\mathsf{m A}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , and lower Tafel slope than those before electrochemical activation. Also, excellent durability was delivered without significant activity loss over continuous operation for $25\mathsf{h}$ , along with a $100\%$ Faradaic yield.
