## 3.4. Mixed metal sites in MOF-derived materials
It has been demonstrated that electrocatalysts with dual or even multiple metal sites showed electrocatalytic performance superior to the counterparts with single metal sites, which can be attributed to the synergistic promotion effects between the different metal sites.[81-82, 151-155] Considering that multivariate metal sites can be uniformly and simultaneously introduced into one MOF framework structure, carbon materials doped with multiple ADMSs can be facilely synthesized using these MOFs incorporated with multiple metal sites as precursors. For instance, Fang and co-workers employed a porphyrin-based MOF with atomically dispersed $\mathsf{F e}^{\mathsf{I I}}$ and $\mathsf{C o}^{\mathsf{\Pi}}$ sites in the porphyrin-based ligands as precursor to afford carbon materials doped with highly dispersed Fe-Nx-C and $\mathsf{C o-N_{x}}$ C sites through high temperature pyrolysis and subsequent acid leaching (Figure 18A-B).[156] The resultant carbon materials had porous and hollow structure that facilitated the mass transport and guarantee the accessibility of the Fe-Nx-C and $\mathsf{C o-N_{x}-C}$ active sites, high graphitization degree of the carbon matrix that promoted the electron transfer, and synergistic promotion effect between the Fe- $\cdot N_{x-C}$ and $\mathsf{C o-N_{x}-C}$ sites. As a result, it showed excellent electrocatalytic performance for ORR in both alkaline and acidic solution than those of $\mathsf{P t/C}$ and the counterparts with single metal sites. This work shows the potential of utilization of multivariate MOFs as templates to afford advanced electrocatalysts with multiple ADMSs, however, corrosive and toxic HF was required in this case in order to remove the aggregated metal materials.
Similar to the aforementioned cases, utilization of Zn-based MOFs as precursors can prevent the aggregation of metal species and avoid the acid leaching step. Following this thinking, Wang and co-workers successfully fabricated carbon materials with atomically dispersed Fe and Co dual sites through one step pyrolysis of a Zn-based MOF incorporated with Fe sources in pores and Co sources in metal nodes without post-treatment (Figure 18C-G).[157] The Fe sources were uniformly introduced in the pores of the $Z n/C o$ bimetallic MOF through double-solvent method, which served as morphology modulators to induce the formation of interior pores in the resultant carbon materials (Figure 18C-D). Aberration corrected HAADF-STEM, M ̈ossbauer spectroscopy, and XAFS analysis confirmed the existence of atomically dispersed Fe-Co dual sites, where the porphyrin-like carbon structure was deformed by the Fe-Co bonds (Figure 18E). The resultant dual ADMSs doped porous carbon materials showed superb electrocatalytic performance for ORR in $0.1~\mathsf{M}$ ${\mathsf{H C l O}}_{4}$ solution with onset and half-wave potentials of 1.06 and $0.863\mathrm{~V~}$ , respectively, along with excellent long-term durability and high tolerance towards methanol and CO. Remarkably, the ${\sf H}_{2}/$ air fuel cell with this dual ADMSs doped carbon as electrocatalyst showed a kinetic current larger than $550\mathsf{m A}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ at $0.6\mathrm{~V~}$ and a peak power density of $>505\mathrm{\mw\cm^{-2}}$ at $0.42\mathrm{~V~}$ , which surpassed most of the reported $\mathsf{P t}$ -free electrocatalysts. DFT simulations were carried out to reveal the reaction mechanisms on the Fe-Co dual sites (Figure 18G). It was shown that the Fe-Co dual sites can provide two anchoring sites for sufficient activation and dissociation of the O-O bonds, which led to a lower energy barrier for O-O bond cleavage and thus a higher electrocatalytic activity for ORR.
So far MOF-derived carbon materials doped with mixed ADMSs are limited to Fe-Co dual sites for ORR. It is expected that carbon materials doped with more than two kinds of ADMSs can be derived from MOFs for other energy conversion applications, as a great diversity of metal sites (including precious and nonprecious metal sites) with different catalytic activities for diverse energy conversion reactions can be incorporated into the MOF precursors.
## 4. Summary and perspective
Pristine MOFs and MOF-derived carbon materials serve as ideal platforms to bridge the gap between homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysis as ADMSs with well-defined configurations can be facilely introduced into the framework structures of MOFs and MOF-derived carbon materials. Various ADMSs have been successfully incorporated into pristine MOFs and MOF-derived carbon materials for energy conversion applications including ORR, OER, HER, and $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ .
For pristine MOFs, ADMSs can be presented in the metal nodes, organic linkers, or pores of MOFs. Coordinatively unsaturated ADMSs in the metal nodes with photocatalytic or electrocatalytic activities are desirable for energy conversion catalysis as they can serve as active sites to adsorb and activate the reactant molecules, which can be intrinsically presented in many MOFs or be produced on the surface of ultrathin 2D MOF nanosheets. In the case of ADMSs in organic linkers of pristine MOFs, well-studied molecular metal complexes with high activities can be incorporated into the linkers of MOFs by using special linkers with bpy or porphyrin units that can coordinate with the metal centers. More than one kind of metal sites or other active components (e.g. Pt nanomaterials and POMs) can be simultaneously incorporated into one MOF crystal to afford multifunctional catalysts with much enhanced performances that result from synergistic effects of these different components. Moreover, anchoring the homogeneous metal complexes in the robust frameworks of MOFs can afford heterogeneous catalysts with improved recyclability and durability without compromising the catalytic activity. Molecular metal complexes can be encaged and isolated by the well-defined pores of MOFs, which prevents the de-activation of the complexes through degradation pathway involving more than one complexes and leads to significantly improved durability.
The ADMSs in pristine MOFs can be transferred to MOFderived carbon materials doped with the corresponding ADMSs through high temperature pyrolysis, as the organic components of MOFs will decompose to carbon at elevated temperature. Compared with ADMSs in pristine MOFs, ADMSs in MOF-derived carbon materials have much improved chemical and thermal stability, higher electrical conductivity, and more hierarchical porosity, which make them promising for electrocatalytic energy conversion catalysis. Normally ZIF-8, a MOF constructed with Zn cations and 2-imidazolates, doped with target metal sites was used as precursor to produce ADMSs in carbon materials as the Zn with relative low boiling point $(907~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ can be easily evaporated out during high temperature pyrolysis, leaving a large amount of free nitrogen sites that can anchor and stabilize the target ADMSs. Carbon nanomaterials with atomically dispersed $C o-N_{x}-C$ , $F e\cdot N_{x}\cdot C$ , and $N i-N_{x}-C$ sites have been successfully synthesized using ZIF-8 as template, which showed excellent electrocatalytic performance for ORR, HER, $C O_{2}\mathsf{R R}$ , and OER in terms of activity and durability. Electrochemical activation of MOF-derived materials to afford ADMSs in carbon materials were also reported.
Although great progress has been made in this field, there is still a long way to go before wide employment and even industrialization of the ADMSs in pristine MOFs and MOF-derived carbon for energy conversion applications. ADMSs in pristine
MOFs encounter the problem of poor chemical stability and low electrical conductivity of most of the traditional MOFs, which requires development of stable and conductive MOFs with novel design and new chemistry to support ADMSs. MOF-derived carbon serves as more stable and conductive substrate to support ADMSs, however, the contents of ADMSs in MOF-derived carbon are limited in order to prevent the aggregation of metal sites. Better understanding of the formation process of ADMSs in MOFderived carbon and new synthetic method are needed to increase the content of the active ADMSs while avoiding the formation of aggregated metal materials. The well-defined configuration of the ADMSs in MOFs and MOF-derived carbon offer great opportunity to reveal the catalytic mechanism on the metal sites at atomic level, while researches reported on experimental or theoretical mechanism studying are still limited. In situ measurements and theoretical calculations that reveal the mechanism of how the reactions happen on the ADMSs are highly desirable, which can provide deep insight and new ideas that can guide future researches to design and synthesize ADMSs in MOFs and MOFderived carbon with desirable properties for more efficient, more environmentally friendly, and more durable energy conversion applications.
